    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/tool-authoring.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Step 4b: A new folder is a named subgraph of its harness — declare it",
      "prov:value": "If what you are adding needs a **new folder**, decide first which of three\nkinds of folder it is. Each has exactly one way to be declared, and the place\nthe folder sits decides which (owner, 2026-10-14):",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-14"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/tool-authoring.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Step 4b: A new folder is a named subgraph of its harness — declare it",
      "prov:value": "1. **A folder directly under the harness root** (a new code area, a new\n   graph): an entry in the harness's own declaration (`<harness>.json`, e.g.\n   `cat-harness.json`), under `directories`, with an `id`, the `path`, its\n   `graphTypologies` and a one-line `description`.\n2. **A folder inside a folder the harness already declares**: declared\n   **from within**, in that directory's own `<dir>.json` (as\n   `skills/skills.json` declares `voices`). This is not a matter of taste:\n   `check:layout-norms` fails a new top-level entry whose path lies inside\n   another declared directory (the pairs it only lists are a baseline). Add `subgraph: true` when the new folder is a subgraph\n   of the INSTANCE rather than a part of its parent's graph (bean `cmsl`:\n   `voices` is the first kind, `beans.json`'s `defs` the second).\n3. **A folder that holds ONE node** — one Tool, one voice: it is **not\n   declared**. It sits inside the declared graph that owns the node and is\n   found through that graph's reader, as each voice folder is found by its\n   `voice.json`. Never declare one Tool's folder as a `tools` directory:\n   discovery loads ONE `tools/index.ts` per harness and refuses a harness\n   that declares two `tools` directories. The Tool's folder is found by\n   that `index.ts` (`toolsFromFolders`), as each voice is by its `voice.json`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cmsl"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/tool-authoring.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Step 4b: A new folder is a named subgraph of its harness — declare it",
      "prov:value": "**One folder per Tool, always** (bean `tdir`, owner 2026-10-15: *\"migrate\nall impacted repos … to sub-folders as is strongly recommended behaviour\"*).\nA Tool is declared in `tools/<id>/<id>.ts` inside the harness's declared\n`tools` directory: the folder, the file stem and the Tool's `id` are one\nstring, the file default-exports `toolFactory((t) => defineTool({ … }))`, and\nthe folder also holds whatever is that Tool's alone (its script, fixtures,\ntests). `tools/index.ts` only calls `toolsFromFolders`\n(`schemas/tools/tool-folders.ts`), which refuses a folder that breaks the\nconvention, so discovery is unchanged. Helpers several Tools share go in\n`tools/lib.ts`, which declares no Tool. A `defineTool(` anywhere else in the\ndirectory is an inline declaration, and `check:tools` reports it.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean tdir",
        "2026-10-15"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/tools/folio-core/tool-authoring.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Step 4b: A new folder is a named subgraph of its harness — declare it",
      "prov:value": "- **A folder nothing declares is a folder nothing reasons about.** No check\n  catches it for you: `check:undeclared-files` sweeps the repository root\n  only, so a new undeclared folder inside a harness passes it.\n- **Declared directories are what can be mounted and exported.** A remote\n  mount lays down declared directories only, so an undeclared code folder is\n  missing downstream (the tools layer's `tools/` was, until 2026-10-14). A\n  harness's package `exports` are derived from its declared, shipped `code`\n  directories (`check:package-exports`, bean `7pvn`), so an undeclared one\n  cannot be imported by package name either. A from-within id cannot yet be\n  mounted on its own: `remote-mount` reads top-level entries only (bean\n  `2j2r`, needed by `mo75`).\n- `bun run cat harness:dirs` then materialises every declared directory, so\n  the folder exists in every checkout rather than only in yours.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 7pvn",
        "2026-10-14"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-14"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
